'''''Sinchewese Community Association v. [[National Police of Balboa|National Police]]''''' (2003–2004) was a {{w|court case}} in [[Balboa]] in which the Sinchewese Community Association challenged the [[National Police of Balboa|National Police]]'s {{w|stop and frisk|stop-and-search}} practices, which disproportionately targeted [[Sinchewese]] individuals in [[Sant Cristòfor]] and [[Portnou]]. The [[National Anti-Discrimination Tribunal]] ruled in favour of the Association, finding that the police's stop-and-search practices constituted indirect {{w|racial discrimination}} under the [[Twelve Guarantees]]. The ruling was the first to address {{w|racial profiling}} by law enforcement and established that police practices producing racially disparate outcomes were actionable under the Guarantees.

== Background ==

The [[Sinchewese]] community in Balboa was concentrated in the harbour-front districts of [[Sant Cristòfor]] and in [[Portnou]], where Sinchewese workers had been employed in port operations, canal construction, and commercial trade since the late nineteenth century. Following reunification, the Sinchewese community experienced a period of economic growth and cultural revival, but also faced persistent stereotyping as outsiders, despite most Sinchewese residents being Balboan citizens by birth.

In the late 1990s and early 2000s, the [[National Police of Balboa|National Police]] introduced a series of stop-and-search protocols in Sant Cristòfor and Portnou as part of an anti-narcotics initiative. The protocols authorised officers to stop and search individuals in designated "high-crime zones" based on "suspicious behaviour" assessments. The Sinchewese Community Association, a civic organisation representing the Sinchewese community, began collecting data on stop-and-search incidents in 2001 after receiving repeated complaints from Sinchewese residents who reported being stopped multiple times per week without cause.

The Association's data, covering the period 2001–2003, demonstrated that Sinchewese individuals were stopped at a rate approximately 3.5 times higher than white Balboan individuals in the same districts, and that Sinchewese individuals were subjected to physical searches at a rate approximately 5 times higher. The data also showed that the "suspicious behaviour" assessments were applied subjectively and that the designation of "high-crime zones" correlated with the geographic concentration of Sinchewese residents.

== Case ==

The Sinchewese Community Association brought a case before the [[National Anti-Discrimination Tribunal]] in 2003, arguing that the stop-and-search practices constituted {{w|racial profiling}} and indirect {{w|racial discrimination}} under the [[Twelve Guarantees]]. The Association's legal team presented the stop-and-search data and argued that the "suspicious behaviour" assessment was a proxy for racial stereotyping and that the "high-crime zone" designation was a proxy for the racial composition of the neighbourhood.

The [[National Police of Balboa|National Police]] defended the stop-and-search protocols on the grounds that they were based on crime data and that the designation of "high-crime zones" reflected actual crime rates, not racial composition. The Police argued that the disparity in stop rates reflected the geographic concentration of crime in certain districts and that the protocols were applied uniformly within each designated zone.

== Ruling ==

The Tribunal ruled in favour of the Sinchewese Community Association in 2004, finding that the stop-and-search practices constituted indirect {{w|racial discrimination}}. The Tribunal held that the "suspicious behaviour" assessment was applied subjectively and that the disparity in stop rates was too large to be explained by crime data alone. The Tribunal found that the designation of "high-crime zones" correlated with the geographic concentration of Sinchewese residents and that the Police had not demonstrated that the designation was based on objective crime data rather than racial composition.

The Tribunal ordered the [[National Police of Balboa|National Police]] to revise its stop-and-search protocols to require objective, documented grounds for each stop, to eliminate the subjective "suspicious behaviour" assessment, and to collect and publish stop-and-search data disaggregated by race. The Tribunal also ordered the Police to report compliance to the Tribunal annually.

== Significance ==

The Sinchewese Community Association case was the first to address {{w|racial profiling}} by law enforcement in Balboa and established that police practices producing racially disparate outcomes were actionable under the [[Twelve Guarantees]]. The ruling was significant for extending the disparate impact principle from employment, housing, and education to policing, and for requiring the collection and publication of stop-and-search data disaggregated by race.

The ruling prompted a nationwide review of police stop-and-search practices and led to the establishment of the Police Complaints Oversight Board in 2006, an independent body responsible for monitoring police compliance with anti-discrimination law.

